     justify-content: space-between;
     align-items: center;
     padding: 5px;
     border-bottom: 1px solid lightgray;
   }

   .close-popup {
     cursor: pointer;
   }

   .modal-body {
     padding: 10px;
     overflow-y: auto;
     height: 80%;
     margin-bottom: 10px;
   }

   .messages p {
     margin: 5px 0;
   }

   .human-message {
     padding: 5px;
     background: #f7f7f7;
     display: inline-block;
     border-radius: 3px;
   }

   .bot-message {
     padding: 5px;
     background: #E1F5FE;
     display: inline-block;
     border-radius: 3px;
     text-align: right;
   }
   ```

4. Connect the chat history button and modal:
   In the HTML, attach a `click` event listener to the chat history button (or the trigger element you created):

   ```
   <button class="chat-history-button" id="show-chat-history">Chat History</button>
   <script>
     document.getElementById("show-chat-history").addEventListener("click", function() {
       document.getElementById("chat-history-modal").style.display = "block";
     });
   </script>
   ```

5. Close the modal upon clicking the close button or outside the modal:
   Add event listeners to the "close-popup" element and the modal container to close the modal:

   ```
   <script>
     let modal = document.getElementById("chat-history-modal");
     let closeBtn = modal.querySelector(".close-popup");

     closeBtn.addEventListener("click", function() {
       modal.style.display = "none";
     });

     modal.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
       if (event.target == modal) {
         modal.style.display = "none";
       }
     });
   </script>
   ```

Now, when a user clicks the "Chat History" button, the modal containing four chat messages (two from the user and two from the bot) will be displayed. The hidden "X" button can be used to close the modal. This setup imitates a modal chat history with a fake chatbot, showcasing previously exchanged messages.

To tell the Type System in TypeScript that a value may have multiple theoretical types but will only have a specific type in practice, you can use a technique known as "type narrowing" or "type assertion." Type narrowing is the process of reducing the possible types of a value based on certain conditions or constraints. Here's how you can achieve this:

1. Union Type: Start by defining a union type that encompasses all the possible types your value may have. Let's assume you have a variable `value` that could potentially be of type `string`, `number`, or `boolean`:
   ```
   let value: string | number | boolean;
   ```
2. Type Guards: Use type guards to narrow down the possible types based on specific conditions. A type guard is a statement or expression that performs a type check and determines the actual type of a value. Here are some examples:

   a. Type Assertions:
      Use type assertions to directly assert the type of `value`:
      ```
      if (typeof value === "string") {
        // Type narrowing to string
        value = value as string;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a string
      } else if (typeof value === "number") {
        // Type narrowing to number
        value = value as number;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a number
      } else if (typeof value === "boolean") {
        // Type narrowing to boolean
        value = value as boolean;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a boolean
      }
      ```
   b. Type Predicates: Write a function that returns a boolean, depending on whether the type of `value` matches the criterion:
      ```
      function isString(value: string | number | boolean): value is string {
        return typeof value === "string";
      }

      function isNumber(value: string | number | boolean): value is number {
        return typeof value === "number";
      }

      function isBoolean(value: string | number | boolean): value is boolean {
        return typeof value === "boolean";
      }
      ```
   With these type predicates, you can narrow the type based on the returned value:
      ```
      if (isString(value)) {
        // Type narrowing to string
        value = value as string;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a string
      } else if (isNumber(value)) {
        // Type narrowing to number
        value = value as number;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a number
      } else if (isBoolean(value)) {
        // Type narrowing to boolean
        value = value as boolean;
        // Proceed with operations assuming `value` is a boolean
      }
      ```

3. Type Casting: Alternatively, you can use type casting to explicitly state the type of `value` after the check. Type casting is simple but less safe than type guards, as it doesn't perform a type check:
   ```
   if (typeof value === "string") {
       value = value as string;
       // Proceed
   } else if (typeof value === "number") {
       value = value as number;
       // Proceed
   } else if (typeof value === "boolean") {
       value = value as boolean;
       // Proceed
   }
   ```

Using either type guards or type casting will inform the TypeScript compiler that `value` only holds the type of the matched condition within each if statement, facilitating more precise type checking and intelligent code completion. Additionally, you can apply these techniques to any value that has a union type and needs to be narrowed down based on specific conditions.

